{"type":"standard","title":"History of tropical cyclone naming","displaytitle":"<span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">History of tropical cyclone naming</span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q15954726","titles":{"canonical":"History_of_tropical_cyclone_naming","normalized":"History of tropical cyclone naming","display":"<span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">History of tropical cyclone naming</span>"},"pageid":27476634,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Hurricane_Kate_%282003%29-_Good_pic.jpg/320px-Hurricane_Kate_%282003%29-_Good_pic.jpg","width":320,"height":218},"originalimage":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Hurricane_Kate_%282003%29-_Good_pic.jpg","width":1000,"height":680},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1245267566","tid":"54b410f8-709e-11ef-8665-493d3298769d","timestamp":"2024-09-12T00:31:20Z","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_tropical_cyclone_naming","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_tropical_cyclone_naming?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_tropical_cyclone_naming?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:History_of_tropical_cyclone_naming"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_tropical_cyclone_naming","revisions":"https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/History_of_tropical_cyclone_naming","edit":"https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_tropical_cyclone_naming?action=edit","talk":"https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:History_of_tropical_cyclone_naming"}},"extract":"The practice of using names to identify tropical cyclones goes back several centuries, with storms named after places, saints or things they hit before the formal start of naming in each basin. Examples of such names are the 1928 Okeechobee hurricane and the 1938 New England hurricane. The system currently in place provides identification of tropical cyclones in a brief form that is easily understood and recognized by the public. The credit for the first usage of personal names for weather systems is given to the Queensland Government Meteorologist Clement Wragge, who named tropical cyclones and anticyclones between 1887 and 1907. This system of naming fell into disuse for several years after Wragge retired, until it was revived in the latter part of World War II for the Western Pacific. Over the following decades, formal naming schemes were introduced for several tropical cyclone basins, including the North and South Atlantic, Eastern, Central, Western and Southern Pacific basins as well as the Australian region and Indian Ocean.","extract_html":"<p>The practice of using names to identify tropical cyclones goes back several centuries, with storms named after places, saints or things they hit before the formal start of naming in each basin. Examples of such names are the 1928 Okeechobee hurricane and the 1938 New England hurricane. The system currently in place provides identification of tropical cyclones in a brief form that is easily understood and recognized by the public. The credit for the first usage of personal names for weather systems is given to the Queensland Government Meteorologist Clement Wragge, who named tropical cyclones and anticyclones between 1887 and 1907. This system of naming fell into disuse for several years after Wragge retired, until it was revived in the latter part of World War II for the Western Pacific. Over the following decades, formal naming schemes were introduced for several tropical cyclone basins, including the North and South Atlantic, Eastern, Central, Western and Southern Pacific basins as well as the Australian region and Indian Ocean.</p>"}